00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.995
around the sentence. Let's check
out how to set it up. First, some context.

7
00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:29.435
The standard RAG pipeline uses
the same text chunk for both

8
00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:34.763
embedding and synthesis. The issue
is that embedding and synthesis. The

9
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:38.463
issue
is that embedding-based retrieval typically

10
00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:42.016
works well with smaller
chunks, whereas the LLM

11
00:00:42.016 --> 00:00:47.270
needs more context and bigger chunks
to synthesize a good answer. What

12
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.784
sentence window retrieval does is
decouple the two a bit. We

13
00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:56.076
first embed smaller chunks or sentences
and store them in

14
00:00:56.076 --> 00:01:00.590
a vector database. We also add
context of the sentences that

15
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.957
occur before and after to
each chunk. During retrieval, we

16
00:01:04.957 --> 00:01:09.915
retrieve the sentences that are
most relevant to the question with

17
00:01:09.915 --> 00:01:12.727
a similarity search
and then replace

18
00:01:12.727 --> 00:01:17.241
the sentence with a full surrounding
context. This allows us

19
00:01:17.241 --> 00:01:20.571
to expand the context
that's actually fed to

20
00:01:20.571 --> 00:01:25.752
the LLM in order to answer
the question. This notebook will introduce

21
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.934
the various components
needed to construct

22
00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:31.820
a sentence window
retriever with Llama

23
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.038
Index. The various components will
be covered in detail.

